IR: Apartment. Yeah, we lived in the same building as my grandparents.

INT: Could you describe your family's economic status at the time?

IR: They both worked. More than that, I don't know.

INT: Was the part of town they lived in, was it a Jewish neighborhood?

IR: No, not that I know of.

INT: Was there anyone else living in your home other than your immediate family?

IR: No.

INT: Did you attend school?

IR: I went to a Jewish nursery school because I was only three at that point.

INT: Do you know if your family observed Jewish rituals in the home?

IR: Very-- a little bit, not very much. I don't remember as a-- as a really young child. Once I came to America, I belonged to the temple, which is the Reform congregation in Atlanta. Or at that time, it was the only one.

INT: Do you know anything about antisemitism, if it existed in your-- in your parents' hometown?

IR: I would guess it did. Otherwise, I don't think we would have had to leave. I don't think you could have been anywhere in Germany without being very much aware of the details of Nazis.

INT: Did your parents ever talk about what motivated them to leave Germany and emigrate?

IR: I don't remember any of those discussions. I can't imagine that they didn't talk about that. They belonged to a program called the New Worlds Club in Atlanta. And I'm sure when those couples got together, they must have discussed all that. Some brought parents. Some couldn't.

INT: During that time, do you know if your parents had enough food to eat?

IR: In Germany? No, once the law came about that Jews could only go shopping at a Jewish-owned store, then it was very limited. And I don't really remember it myself. I just remember my mother telling me that we had my mother's-- my mother's father lived in a little village called Jagstfeld. And they owned a grocery store. And they had a huge garden in the background, and they would bring us food several times. The town, Jagstfeld, is about an hour from Frankfurt. And they would bring food to Frankfurt.

INT: And what were their names?

IR: That family? Oh, it was my aunt, Ida [? Messner ?]

INT: What attempts did your family make to leave Germany?

IR: What attempts? My father learned from someone that he had a relative in America. And he was-- I don't know whether he had already started taking English lessons, but the-- whoever he was with wrote-- Daddy wrote the letter in German, and he-- and this other person would translate for him. And those letters were sent to-- it was just addressed to Mrs. Rosovsky in New York. And how that got delivered at the time, I don't know. She was involved with the National Council of Jewish Women and HIAS. And that started the quest for coming to America.

INT: What happened to your family assets?

IR: I'm sorry?

INT: Your family's assets. Your home, your belongings?

IR: They were-- we came in April of '38, and we were allowed to bring-- the household, whatever they wanted to bring.

INT: Who emigrated with you?

IR: My father, myself, and Mother.

INT: How old were you at that time?

IR: I was three, almost four. I was born in June, and we came in April.

INT: Where did you go?

IR: We left Germany from Hamburg on the SS President Harding. And we sailed to New York. And the family that sponsored us came to New York to meet us there. I think that was a rule that whoever sponsored you or a representative had to meet the family. And I don't remember how long we stayed in New York, maybe a day or so, and then took the train to Atlanta.

INT: When you left from Hamburg, was that a direct journey?

IR: Yes.

INT: Did you have to pay for your own tickets for the ship?

IR: I don't know.

INT: What was the journey like?

IR: I don't remember any of that. I do remember my parents bought me a little sailor doll and-- kind of beat up. I kept it for years and years and years and recently donated it to the Holocaust Museum with some other items from my family.

INT: The person who sponsored you, what was his reason for sponsoring you?

IR: Reaching out, because my dad reached out and he responded almost immediately. It was within a very short time. I think the correspondence started in January of '37, and we were in America in April of '38. And that was before telephones or air mail. I mean, you know, it was all ship mail at that time.

INT: He was not a relative?

IR: Yes. Yes, he was a distant cousin, a distant cousin. My father and my mother were in someone's home, complaining that they didn't have any sponsors. And someone reminded my father that a great aunt of his had married an American. And all they knew was her name, and she married an Alexander. And that was the information that my dad sent to Mrs. Rosovsky at HIAS. And within a very short-- and he knew that they lived in Atlanta. So they got in touch with the temple in Atlanta. And it just so happened that my-- the son of the man that sponsored us, Alex Dittler, was president of the congregation, and his wife was vice president of the congregation. And they just put it all together.

INT: What family members were left behind in Germany?

IR: Everyone. We were the only ones that came to America. My Catholic family stayed in Germany. My grandfather was picked up by Nazis because his daughter had married a Jew. He had to prove at one point that he was Catholic. He was beaten up by the Nazis and suffered a stroke at that time, but recovered. And they came to America for a couple of years later.

All of my-- no, a cousin of my father's lived and came to America. But everybody else died, at Auschwitz, probably.

Yeah, their family name's on the wall outside-- no, outside the Jewish cemetery in Frankfurt.

INT: How was your family received by the community, the Jewish community in Atlanta?

IR: So welcoming, so helpful in every single way. There were several families that belonged to the temple beside the Dittlers, who-- he was president of the congregation. They had several good friends that all just became our family's mentor. I was driven to Sunday school. I was driven to school. I was driven wherever we needed to go. My parents were picked up twice a week to go to the-- at that time, it was called the Jewish Alliance, which ended up being Jewish Community Center-- for English lessons twice a week. Just in every way very helpful in-- that I know of, that I could see, that I would remember.

INT: I just want to go back to any family members that were still left in Germany. Do you know if anyone witnessed violence or the Kristallnacht event?

IR: My grandmother traveled to the ship on Kristallnacht. And she talks about seeing fires everywhere. And she traveled by herself to the ship. And somewhere along the line, her coat was stolen, so she traveled without a coat in November. Other than that, that was just really never discussed.

INT: How did your parents assimilate?

IR: I never heard anybody complaining. They-- all they wanted to do was be American. That I do remember. I mean, I-- the radio was always on English.

No, I think-- I think it was very hard for my mother. I think it was harder for my mother because she left her parents. She left her grandparents.

But I never-- they never complained. We just didn't hear it.

INT: Were you aware that they shared any of their experiences with anyone?

IR: No.

INT: How did you learn the language?

IR: When I went to school here.

INT: How were you treated when you went to school?

IR: I don't really remember, other than the fact that I really didn't want to speak German around my parents. I didn't want to be known that I was their child, you know. I just wanted to speak English.

INT: Why didn't you want to be with them when they spoke German?

IR: Because at that time, people didn't mention American Jews or German Jews. You just heard the word German was Nazi.

INT: Do you remember when your family learned about what was happening to the Jewish people in Europe?

IR: When they learned after-- you mean after the war?

INT: After they settled here, during the years of the Holocaust, were they aware of what was happening to family members in Europe?

IR: Yes, like everybody else, through newspapers. And although I think my-- for a long time, you couldn't correspond. And then after the war, you could have that correspondence again. And that's-- I'm sure that's the only way they knew, other than what was in the newspaper.

INT: How did they find out about their family members that were murdered in Auschwitz?

IR: I think that came through a cousin named [? Resha ?] Parker, who lived in New York. Her parents were killed.

Just by phone or letters or however the correspondence was. I think that was the only way that we knew for sure.

INT: What type of education did you receive?

IR: I went to high school and then one year of business school after that.

INT: What was your occupation?

Did you--

IR: As a-- as an adult? Oh, I was lucky enough to be a mother, a stay-at-home mom at the time. Then, when my youngest child was in eighth grade, I started working for a travel agency. And that was really fun. My husband and I were lucky enough to travel a lot, and the first trip that we made was spending a month in Alaska. And when I returned, I happened to be friends of a couple who owned an agency, Allied World Travel. And I used to tease my friend, if you need any help on Alaska, I'll be happy to help. And one day she called, and she said, would you consider taking 34 farmers to Alaska? And I just was shocked. I said, I don't know. I have to ask my husband, who was in New York at the time. I said, I've never checked into a hotel myself. I mean, these were-- you know, this was in the years that many women did not travel by themselves or whatever. And I called my husband in New York to tell him. And I said I was really so nervous about doing something like this. And all he said to me is, you can read English. You can do anything. So I did that for about 10 years, traveled all over the world with farming-- groups who were farmers. And that was so fascinating. I mean, to think that-- I kept thinking, here I am, this young Jewish princess, traveling to farms all over the world and learning what was the-- one of the most important things in our lives, food. So that's how I started. And then-- and then I worked for Tales and Toys, a wonderful department-- toy store in Highland Park. And then, for a couple of years, I worked at the Highland Park Stationery Station and eventually retired.

INT: How many years did you spend in Atlanta?

IR: I was almost 19 when I got married, so from the time I was four to the time I got married.

INT: Growing up in Atlanta, did your parents observe any Jewish traditions in the home?

IR: I don't really remember. I remember going to religious school and high holiday services. And I remember sometimes they would go to what at that time was really Sunday school-- Sunday services. And I don't really remember too much.

INT: Do you remember celebrating any Jewish holidays?

IR: Yes, all the Jewish holidays. And at that time, I went to religious school-- we call it Sunday school-- and was confirmed. And what I do remember as a younger child was how we had all of our Seders, every year, at the temple. And that was really interesting because at that time, I really didn't know that some people have two Seders or anything more Orthodox traditions. And in Atlanta, at the Reform congregation, there was one Seder. Most people had one Seder. And we had it at the temple, which was wonderful. And that was prepared by women in the congregation, their cooks, because many families had help, their cooks. And I-- that was a really happy time.

INT: Did you have a favorite Jewish holiday?

IR: No, not really.

INT: Do you--

IR: I observe them all today, you know. I'm very much involved with our congregation here.

INT: Do you remember celebrating Hanukkah when you were growing up?

IR: I really don't remember that. No, I think Passover was probably the most important at that time to our family. And we were on very limited income, so it wouldn't have been anything crazy, like it is today.

INT: Was there anything special that your mother prepared for the holidays?

IR: I don't remember.

INT: Were you married?

IR: I'm sorry?

INT: Did you get married?

IR: I got married in Atlanta in 1953.

INT: What was your husband's name?

IR: Kurt L. Rothschild.

INT: Did he have a middle name?

IR: It was Ludwig, given to him by his parents. And he hated that because it was so German. And when he came to America, he legally changed his name. So he was always known as Kurt L. Rothschild.

INT: Did he ever talk about his experiences before he emigrated?

IR: Somewhat, just that he wasn't allowed to finish school. He grew up in a town named Friedberg, which is about an hour from Frankfurt. It was a very wealthy family, and they traveled a great deal. And he just had a very privileged life in Germany. And so he went to trade school and-- because that's-- he was allowed to do that. And he went in. It was leather business that he was in. My grandmother, his mother's family was-- my mother-in-law, I should say, was in the leather business. And so he trained in handbags. That was in Offenbach, Germany.

INT: How did you meet your husband?

IR: We met in the Catskills, which was really fun. I worked as a counselor at a German-Jewish resort hotel, which is no longer there anymore. It was called the Rose Garden. And so I worked as a counselor. There were two gals who worked as counselors. And at that time, families would go on vacation in-- but not be with the children, like they all are today. And so we had-- there was a camp where we-- it was like day camp for the children. And we took care of the kids. And the last weekend of that year, I met Kurt in the parking lot of the hotel. And I don't know why, but the owner asked me to come over and meet this-- these people. It was my husband and-- his brother and his sister-in-law had just come from Switzerland. And the owner of the hotel knew the family. And that's how we met.

INT: Can you describe your wedding?

IR: My wedding was at the Standard Club in Atlanta. It was a luncheon wedding, [PAUSES] just a lovely luncheon wedding. [PAUSES] There were probably about 20 people who were at the wedding-- family, very little family because we had very little family. My grandmother was still living, and she was at the wedding. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] One of the Dittler daughters was my maid of honor. My family insisted that she be the maid of honor rather than my really good friend, which I felt sorry about for so many years. But they felt that they owed this to the family.

INT: Do you have any children?

IR: I have three children.

INT: What are their names?

IR: Bruce, Steven, and Kathy.

INT: When was Bruce born?

IR: Bruce was born April the 4th, 19-- oh, my gosh. '64.

INT: Where was he born?

IR: Wait a minute. Hold on a minute. 19-- I have to look that up. He was born '56. I'm sorry, April the 4th, 1956. And then-- I'm sorry. You were going to--

INT: Where was he born?

IR: He was born in New York.

Then I have Kathy, who was born August the 17th, 1956, and then Steven, who was born in-- all three were born in New York. And Steven was born May the 2nd, 1955.

INT: How did you choose your children's names?

IR: Well, we had a friend who had a son named Bruce, and we just loved the name. His middle name is Nathan, which was after my grandfather. Kathy is Lynn. And we just liked the name Lynn. And then Steven, his middle name is Mark, who was for my father.

INT: What kind of education did your children receive?

IR: All college.

INT: When did you tell them that you were a Holocaust survivor?

IR: I don't think I ever told them that. I mean, I-- as I said before, I just have never considered myself a survivor because I have lived such a fabulous, wonderful life here. And I didn't really experience things. Probably reacted a lot because of my family, but I don't think we ever really discussed that.

INT: Are you involved in any Holocaust survivor organizations?

IR: Well, I'm a member of the-- a charter member of the Holocaust Museum in Washington. Holocaust Memorial Museum in Washington, and then a member here at the Illinois museum. But I just-- my major time is spent with temple activities.

INT: Did your children get married?

IR: Yes. Yes. You know, I-- let me go back and just say something about being a Holocaust survivor or when we talk to our children about the Holocaust. I don't remember talking about them very much about that. But my husband and I were invited back to Germany three times by his home town. Once, it was an-- it was a town organization that sponsored this. It wasn't a German government, but it was the city that sponsored the trips for people who had to leave for either political or religious reasons. And the first trip was because-- it was a first reunion of his classmate. At that time, they all celebrated-- well, they may still do, I don't know-- reunions for their communion year. And of course, Kurt was in school at that time. So that was the first reunion, was the communion year. The second was to celebrate--

I think they were doing a memorial for rebuilding the synagogue-- not rebuilding the synagogue, but dedicating the area where the synagogue stood before it was burned down. And the third trip was the 50th anniversary. And so that's when we really started talking to our kids about that. It's probably the first they knew. It was just there. You know, it was just one of those things that we never really discussed again.

INT: How did it feel to go back to Germany?

IR: Awkward at first, and very welcoming. I went back in 2020 with my sister.

I don't think that that the people that-- I went back because of the city of-- Germany brought back people. But I don't think that people were ever trying to apologize. I think that they just accepted the fact, horrible as it was, and just wanted to make life a little easier for people.

INT: Were you or your husband able to visit the home that you were born in?

IR: Yes. Yes, several times. We were lucky enough to travel a lot, and we took our kids on several trips to Europe. And we did a roots trip. And we went to the town that my husband grew up in. But we-- I think we probably have done that four times in my lifetime. And his home is a really beautiful, beautiful, elegant home. And we saw that town. We were walking on the street one day. And someone looks at my husband and says, Kurt? Yeah, quite a shock. And it was someone that he had known. And then also, they had really wonderful neighbors in his town. Since they lived in a home, it was different than living in an apartment. And the former neighbors who helped his family on Kristallnacht invited us over to their home. And he had been, I think, in the German Army, but very, very-- nothing to do with the Nazis. But I think everybody had to serve no matter what they thought. And we were entertained in their home, which was lovely. That was one of the first trips that we made with our kids. And I mean, there were-- a lot of people were very nice.

INT: What is the religion of your children's spouses?

IR: Two-- let's see. One converted. Amy's husband converted. The others are-- some are not Jewish. I'm not sure what religion it might be. All right. I'm trying to think of my son's family. [PAUSES] One child-- one grandchild and his wife are not Jewish. One, he converted to become a Baptist, I believe.

His second daughter is married to someone who may be half Jewish. And the third, Amy's husband, Jeffrey, converted to Judaism. And in my daughter's family, [PAUSES] let's see. Everyone is not Jewish. The spouses are not Jewish. And my youngest grandson, Sam, who lives in Boston, is not married.

INT: How many grandchildren do you have?

IR: I have 11 and almost 12. The 12th one is due this week. Very exciting.

INT: Do you have any great-grandchildren?

IR: I'm sorry. That's what I-- I thought that's what you were asking about, great-grandchildren.

INT: So I had asked, how many grandchildren do you have?

IR: Oh, I have seven.

INT: And--

IR: Wait a minute. Let me make sure that-- seven grandchildren.

INT: What are the names of your grandchildren?

IR: [LAUGHING] I'm glad I wrote this down. Bruce, who is my oldest child, has Amy, Diane and Scott. Kathy has Daniel, David, and Laura. And Steven has Sam. Those are my grandchildren.

INT: What are your great-grandchildren's names?

IR: Oh.

Amy has Benjamin Kurt Albertson. Diane has Jackson Leland Kaplan Scott is Luke Alexander Rothschild, Hudson Scott Rothschild, and Sawyer John Rothschild.

OK, that's Bruce's children. Kathy has Daniel. Daniel has Amy Marie Lettenberger Klein and Samantha Ann Lettenberger Klein-- they were born twins on April of '17-- and then William David Lettenberger Klein. And they live in Ohio. David is Kathy's second child. And he has Parker Robert Klein and Hayes Daniel Klein. And they live in California. And Laura and her husband just recently moved back to Illinois. And their son's name is Nash James Slinker. And Sam is not married.

INT: What does it feel like to have such a large family?

IR: Oh, my gosh. Amazing, amazing. I just remember one thing of my childhood-- not even of my childhood, but young-- having a young family. We always go to Atlanta for Thanksgiving, the entire family. And I remember when my parents were still living, that one time my mother looked at my father. And she always called him Artur, which is Arthur. And she said, Artur, just look at this, from two German immigrants. And that time we were only about 30 people. I haven't never-- I haven't counted since then, so.

INT: Where did you go after you left Atlanta?

IR: We got married and went to New York, lived-- and lived in New York for maybe nine years. And then we moved to Chicago because that was my husband's area of travel.

INT: Did you ever experience discrimination?

IR: I think visibly, once, when-- oh, actually, a second time too. But once, the first time ever, I was on one of the travels with the farmers, and somebody said something about being anti-Jewish. And I just went livid. Here I was, a travel-- a travel manager. But I said to him, I'm Jewish, and I really resent your feelings. I'm sorry I didn't go into it more with him, but I was just-- I didn't. And then, at another time, I was discussing shopping with a woman that worked for me. And she said something about jewing them down. And I, but very nicely, explained to her that's not a right term to use. And we talked about that. But honestly, that was the only time that I can really remember. But I do remember them, you know. I mean, obviously, it sticks in your mind.

INT: When your grandmother arrived, who had witnessed Kristallnacht, how was her adjustment?

IR: It was hard, but-- and she came to-- she lived with us for a few years. And then she went on to become the governess of a family, one of the families that helped our family. It was just very difficult. This is the grandmother that sat shiva for my father. And then he brought her to America. She and my mother were never close, never, which I certainly understand. But she was a wonderful grandmother. We used to visit her a lot when she lived close, not too close by, but we would visit her.

INT: Have you ever visited the State of Israel?

IR: Yes, probably a dozen times, 10 times, probably.

INT: What does the State of Israel mean to you?

IR: I'm really pleased that there is a home for Jews. I am not pleased at all with the political problems of today. I went to Israel many years ago for the first time. My husband had parents who-- a aunt and uncle who lived in Haifa, and that was a wonderful experience. Just we were there for a month. We had a guide. We went all over at that time. I think it was about '69 or early '70 somewhere. And that was wonderful, a totally different atmosphere at that time than what you see today. One of the cousins, my husband's cousin, was a physician. And he took care of-- oh, I can't remember the--

I can't remember the name. But they were-- they were not Arab, [PAUSES] but a tribal people. And it was just wonderful. We were invited to their home. And it was quite an experience. And then, in 2010, I met someone, and he was my significant other. And his sister lives in Israel. And so we went every year to see them, and that was very exciting. So the last time I was in Israel was in 2019.

INT: What was his name?

IR: Edgar Rose.

INT: How do you practice Judaism today?

IR: Well, I observe Sabbath by ritual. I-- by-- I light the candles and say my prayers. But I'm not-- I don't observe the day of silence really-- not day of silence, but quietness. Go to religious services. I'm very much involved with the temple. For years, I was on the Board of Trustees. I did various committees on the-- of the temple board. And now I-- because of my age, I just stopped ushering. This next service will be the first time that I'm not ushering for 20 years. I feel a little awkward about that. I still bake once a month for a program to-- for homeless children. I still do Feed the Homeless, which is preparing food for several organizations downtown.

INT: Do you read books or see films about the Holocaust?

IR: I stopped doing that a few years ago. I just couldn't read it anymore. You know, we all have a story. Everybody's got a different story. And I just thought, I don't need to hear any more.

INT: How were you affected by Adolf Eichmann's trial in Israel?

IR: I think I was too young to really pay close attention. I don't remember the year that that was, but we-- I remember watching everything. And I mean, it was so appalling. Everything-- I mean, I don't know how else to describe it-- inhumane.

INT: Did you follow John Demjanjuk's trial?

IR: No. [PAUSES] You know, I'm very much aware of that. But I didn't want to read all the details.

INT: Why did you come forward to tell your story today?

IR: Well, I got a phone call from my son, who said he had read, I think, either email or saw something in the newspaper. And he called me and said, I did something today that I think I should have asked your opinion of. And he-- and then he told me that he had contacted this whatever news item that he saw. And I said, no, that's perfectly fine. I don't think I would have ever come on my own. And I really thank him. And I think that evening I got a phone call from someone. I mean, that evening-- I was really surprised that it was so fast. And then, within a couple of weeks, I heard from you.

INT: What would you say is your greatest triumph over Hitler?

IR: Living. Living an unbelievably grateful, wonderful life.

INT: Is there anything else that you'd like to add?

IR: No. I'm just grateful to be an American, despite our politics today, free, and do whatever I want to do. I-- as I say, I've lived a-- what I consider a very privileged, wonderful life.

INT: What would you like to say to future generations?

IR: [SIGHING] I would just have to say what I said when I was in Germany. I-- when I was there in 2020, I was asked to speak to high school students. And that question was always asked of me at that time. And I would just say, be kind, be gracious, keep your eyes open. And I laugh about this because I used to say it all before it became a popular thing. If you see something, say something. That's what I would just say. Keep your eyes open for all those around you.

INT: Thank you, Inge.

IR: You're very welcome.

